  <running_head>DE ETYMOLOGIA.</running_head>
  <bottom_margin>
    <signature>B 2</signature>
  </bottom_margin>
  <main_text>
    <l n="1">
      <i>Excipe</i> dualia in ω terminata, quæ et-</l>
    <l n="2">iam contracta acuuntur. ut: νόω, νῶ, à νόος:</l>
    <l n="3">νῶι, νώ, ab ἐγώ. Accusativum item quartę</l>
    <l n="4">declinationis Contractorum, à Nomi-</l>
    <l n="5">nativo in ω.ut: Λητόα, Λητώ. ]</l>
    <l n="6" type="heading" level="1" para="true">DE ETYMOLOGIA.</l>
    <l n="7" para="true">
      <i>Partes orationis</i>, Græcis sunt novem:</l>
    <l n="8">Articulus, Nomen, Pronomen, Ver-</l>
    <l n="9">bum, Participium, Adverbium, Præpo-</l>
    <l n="10">sitio, Conjunctio, &amp; Interjectio.</l>
    <l n="11" para="true">[Vulgò tamen Grammatici, Interjec-</l>
    <l n="12">tionem inter Adverbia numerant.]</l>
    <l n="13" para="true">Ex his variabiles sunt quinque prio-</l>
    <l n="14">res: posteriores quatuor invariabiles.</l>
    <l n="15" para="true">Accidentia illarum eadem sunt, quæ</l>
    <l n="16">Latinis: nisi quòd in Numero &amp;</l>
    <l n="17">Casu quædam diversitas est.</l>
    <l n="18" para="true">
      <i>Numeri</i> Græcis sunt tres: Singularis,</l>
    <l n="19">Dualis,</l>
    <l n="20">&amp; Pluralis.</l>
    <l n="21" para="true">Singularis, qui de uno tantùm</l>
    <l n="22">loquitur. ut: ὁ ἄνθρωπος.</l>
    <l n="23" para="true">Dualis, qui de duobus tantùm lo-</l>
    <l n="24">quitur. ut: τὼ ἀνθρώπω.</l>
    <l n="25" para="true">Pluralis, qui de pluribus, quàm duo-</l>
    <l n="26">bus, loquitur. ut: οἱ ἄνθρωποι.</l>
    <l n="27" para="true">
      <i>Casus</i> Græcis sunt quinque: Nomi-</l>
    <l n="28">nati-</l>
  </main_text>
